{"name":"Silk for the Gods","hex":"#ecddc9","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fine fiber excreted by the silkworm or other arthropod (such as a spider).","A thread or yarn made from silk fibers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To develop silk.","To remove the silk from (corn)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silk"},{"term":"for","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Fore."]},{"part":"Conjunction","definitions":["Because."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/for"},{"term":"gods","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of god"]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Ellipsis of oh gods."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gods"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silk","blocks":[{"text":"Silk is a natural protein fiber, some forms of which can be woven or knitted into textiles. The protein fiber of silk is composed mainly of fibroin (75-80%) and a smaller coating of sericin (20-25%). It is most commonly produced from the cocoons of certain insect larvae. The best-known silk is obtained from the cocoons of the larvae of the mulberry silkworm Bombyx mori, which are reared in captivity (sericulture)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Silk_raw_01a.jpg/330px-Silk_raw_01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Silk_raw_01a.jpg/500px-Silk_raw_01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Silk_raw_01a.jpg/1280px-Silk_raw_01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Silk_raw_01a.jpg/500px-Silk_raw_01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut